(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-035"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-035","title":"DVRUO Classes CS | DS | Asymptotic Notation & Loop Time Complexity | Weekly Quiz 1","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS | DS | Asymptotic Notation & Loop Time Complexity | Weekly Quiz 1","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":35,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2160,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"DS"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/050_exam_1081_GO Classes CS DS Asymptotic Notation & Loop Time Complexity Weekly Quiz 1.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a problem: Given an array, check whether the array is in sorted order or not. Suppose both iterative and recursive approaches are used to solve this problem.</p><p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Recursive approach gives space complexity as $O(n)$ for recursive stack space.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Iterative approach gives Auxiliary space as $\\Theta(n).$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Time Complexity for recursive approach is $O(n).$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Time Complexity for iterative approach is $\\Theta(n \\log n).$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"377630\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Option A</p>\n\n<p><em>For n elements,an recursive approach takes O(n) space complexity because of n function calls(or Recursive calls).</em></p>\n\n<p><em>Option B</em></p>\n\n<p><em>To check whether an array is sorted or not,an iterative approach just need space complexity of O(1),so no need for auxillary space of O(n)</em></p>\n\n<p>Option C</p>\n\n<p><em>This is just a simple checking problem,n recursive calls just take time complexity of O(n)</em></p>\n\n<p>Option D</p>\n\n<p><em>To check whether array is sorted or not(just checking,not sorting) &nbsp;look at every element –&nbsp;O(n)&nbsp;</em></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><em>Option A&amp;C are correct.</em></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"408485\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Option A,C</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"1000\" src=\"media/81/81b978071e43e25018a5.jpg\" style=\"float:left\" width=\"1000\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"493364\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><pre>def isSorted(arr, start=0, end=None):\n&nbsp; &nbsp; if end is None:\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; end = len(arr)\n&nbsp; &nbsp; if end - start &lt; 2:\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; return True\n&nbsp; &nbsp; mid = (start + end) // 2\n&nbsp; &nbsp; return (arr[mid - 1] &lt;= arr[mid]) and isSorted(arr, start, mid) and isSorted(arr, mid, end)\n but this has a space complexity of logn</pre></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"376006\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">why b and d option is wrong can anyone explain me</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"482536\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Hi<br>\nOne analysis made from this question:-<br>\nThis question is asking about tc/sc of wether array is sorted or not......it is not asking about tc/sc of sorting the array i mistakenly assumed the second one</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Rank the following functions by increasing order of growth. That is, find any arrangement $g 1, g 2, g 3, g 4$ of the functions satisfying $g 1=O(g 2), g 2=O(g 3), g 3=$ $\\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g} 4)$.<br>$$<br>\\begin{aligned}<br>&amp;f_{1}(n)=2^{(\\log n)^{4}}<br>&amp;f_{2}(n)=\\pi^{n}<br>&amp;f_{3}(n)=n^{5(\\log n)^{2}}<br>&amp;f_{4}(n)=\\sqrt{2^{\\sqrt{n}}}<br>\\end{aligned}<br>$$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$f 1, f 3, f 4, f 2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$f 3, f 1, f 4, f 2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$f 1, f 3, f 2, f 4$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$f 1, f 3, f 4, f 2$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 19)</small></h4><a name=\"375725\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer will be option : B&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>we will see why</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b45371fb9e103ee022ee.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"375629\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer : Option B</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Assume log base is 2</strong></p>\n\n<p>$f_{3}(n)= n^{5\\left ( \\log n \\right )^{2}}= (2^{\\log n})^{5(\\log n)^{2}}=2^{5(\\log n)^{3}}$-----(1)</p>\n\n<p>From (1)&nbsp;$f_{3}\\left ( n \\right )\\leq f_{1}\\left ( n \\right )\\Rightarrow f_{3}\\left ( n \\right )= O(f_{1}\\left ( n \\right ))$</p>\n\n<p>$f_{4}(n)= 2^{\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{2}}$-----(2)</p>\n\n<p>From (2)&nbsp;$f_{1}\\left ( n \\right )\\leq f_{4}\\left ( n \\right )\\Rightarrow f_{1}\\left ( n \\right )= O(f_{4}\\left ( n \\right ))$</p>\n\n<p>$f_{4}\\left ( n \\right )\\leq f_{2}(n)$ since&nbsp;$2^{\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{2}}\\leq 3.14^{n}$ (&nbsp;π is approx equal to 3.14)$2^{\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{2}}\\leq 3.14^{n}\\Rightarrow f_{4}(n)= O(f_{2}(n))$</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore f3,f1,f4,f2$ are in increasing order of growth</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375601\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What will be the time complexity of $\\text{function()}?$</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">function(int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        for (int j = i; j &lt; i * i; j++) {\n            if (j % i == 0) {\n                for (int k = 0; k &lt; j; k++) {\n                    printf(\"*\");\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}    \n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\Theta \\left(n^{3}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\Theta\\left(n^{4}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\Theta\\left(n^{5}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\Theta\\left(n^{2} \\log n\\right)$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 11)</small></h4><a name=\"375616\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><small>Let us first analyze for how many times the given if&nbsp;condition gets true and enters into the $k^{th}$ loop :-</small></p>\n\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee;border:1px solid #cccccc;padding:5px 10px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;if (j % i == 0)</div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:533px\">\n\t<tbody>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:129px\">$i^{th}$ value</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;0</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:53px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;1</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;2</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:51px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;3</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:47px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;-</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:52px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; -</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:56px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; n</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:129px\">range for which $j^{th}$loop will get executed</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;0</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:53px\">&nbsp; 1 to 1</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; 2 to 4</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:51px\">&nbsp;3 to 9</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:47px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;-</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:52px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; -</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:56px\">&nbsp; n to $n^{2}$</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:129px\">number of times the if condition gets true</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;0</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:53px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;0&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:50px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;1</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:51px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;2</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:47px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;-</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:52px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; -</td>\n\t\t\t<td style=\"width:56px\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;n-1</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>NOTE :- in the $j^{th}$ loop the condition does not involve $”=”$ therefore “if condition” is true for exactly $(i-1)$ times.</strong></p>\n\n<p>So as we analyzed for $i^{th}$ iteration the if condition will be true for exactly $(i-1)$ times.</p>\n\n<p>now lets analyze the $k^{th}$ loop :-</p>\n\n<p>TC of $j^{th}$ loop will be =$\\theta(j)$</p>\n\n<p>Now lets analyze the $j^{th}$ and $k^{th}$ loops combinely :-</p>\n\n<p>The if condition will be true when the value of $j$ will be $= i,2i,3i,4i,------,(i-1)i$</p>\n\n<p>Which means when $j=i$ the condition got true and the $k^{th}$ loop will run for $i$ times as $j=i$ and we know that $k^{th}$ loop runs for&nbsp;$\\theta(j)$ so it will run for $i$ times.Similarly for $j=2i$ when condition got true the $k^{th}$ loop will run for $2i$ times and so on….</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; So combined TC of both&nbsp;$j^{th}$ and $k^{th}$ loops is&nbsp;&nbsp;= $i+2i+3i+ –&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp; +(i-1)i$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;= $i(1+2+3+ –&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;+ (i-1))$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;= $i(\\frac{(i^{2}-i)}{2})$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;= $\\theta(i^{3})$</p>\n\n<p>so the TC for $j^{th}$ and $k^{th}$ combined is $\\theta(i^{3})$</p>\n\n<p>Now lets analyze the $i^{th}$loop:-</p>\n\n<p>$i^{th}$ loop runs for $\\theta(n)$ times and we just calculate the combined TC of&nbsp;of both&nbsp;$j^{th}$ and $k^{th}$ loops which is&nbsp;$\\theta(i^{3})$ so these both combined&nbsp;$j^{th}$ and $k^{th}$ loop will run for $n$ times.</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore$ So overall TC for the code will be =$1^{3}+2^{3}+3^{3}+ –&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;–&nbsp;+n^{3}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; =$(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2})^{2}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; =$\\theta(n^{4})$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore$&nbsp; &nbsp; Final TC of the code is = $\\theta(n^{4})$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"375746\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer to this question is option B</p>\n\n<p>we will see why</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/559c92ada60b7e373825.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"493856\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let me know is it right?&nbsp; &nbsp;<img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"900\" src=\"media/7d/7d55238fa764d84832c7.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"494789\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><br>Hope this helps&nbsp;<br><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"667\" src=\"media/64/6420d893fdb494f4baa6.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"499304\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">To calculate the time complexity of the given function, let's analyze the loops and their execution costs step-by-step.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\"><b>1. Analyze the Loop Structure</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\">The code consists of three nested parts:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b>Outer Loop:</b> <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)</code></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1,0,0\">Variable <code>i</code> runs from 0 to <code>n-1</code>. (We can effectively treat it as 1 to <code>n</code> for asymptotic analysis, avoiding the division by zero case at <code>i=0</code>).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b>Middle Loop:</b> <code>for (int j = i; j &lt; i * i; j++)</code></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,1,0,0\">Variable <code>j</code> runs from <code>i</code> up to <code>i*i</code>.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,0\"><b>Condition:</b> <code>if (j % i == 0)</code></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,1,0,0\">The code inside the <code>if</code> block only runs when <code>j</code> is a multiple of <code>i</code>.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,0\"><b>Innermost Loop:</b> <code>for (int k = 0; k &lt; j; k++)</code></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1,0,0\">This loop performs <code>j</code> operations (printing <code>*</code>).</p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><b>2. Calculate the Work for a Single Iteration of <code>i</code></b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">Let's fix the value of <code>i</code> and calculate the total work done.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,0\">The middle loop iterates <code>j</code> from <code>i</code> to <code>i*i</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,0\">The condition <code>j % i == 0</code> means we only care about values of <code>j</code> that are multiples of <code>i</code>.</p></li><li><p>In the range $[i, i^2)$, the multiples of i are:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$1 \\cdot i, \\quad 2 \\cdot i, \\quad 3 \\cdot i, \\quad \\dots, \\quad (i-1) \\cdot i$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>(Note: The loop condition is j &lt; i*i, so the last multiple is $(i-1)i$, not $i^2$.)</p></li><li><p>For each of these valid j values, the innermost loop runs j times. So, we sum up these values:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$Work(i) = (1 \\cdot i) + (2 \\cdot i) + (3 \\cdot i) + \\dots + ((i-1) \\cdot i)$$</p></li><li><p>Factor out i:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$Work(i) = i \\cdot [1 + 2 + 3 + \\dots + (i-1)]$$</p></li><li><p>The sum inside the brackets is the sum of the first $i-1$ integers, which is approximately $\\frac{i^2}{2}$.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$Work(i) \\approx i \\cdot \\frac{i^2}{2} = \\frac{i^3}{2}$$</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"7\">So, for each <code>i</code>, the complexity is roughly <b>$\\Theta(i^3)$</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"8\"><b>3. Calculate Total Complexity</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"9\">Now, we sum this work across the outer loop as <code>i</code> goes from 1 to <code>n</code>.</p><p>$$Total\\ Time = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} Work(i)$$</p><p>$$Total\\ Time \\approx \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{i^3}{2}$$</p><p>$$Total\\ Time \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} i^3$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"13\">We know that the sum of cubes $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i^3$ is proportional to $n^4$ (specifically, it equals $(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2})^2$).</p><p>$$Total\\ Time \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\Theta(n^4) = \\Theta(n^4)$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"15\"><b>Conclusion</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"16\">The dominant term is $n^4$. Therefore, the time complexity is $\\Theta(n^4)$.</p><p>Correct Option:</p><p>B. $\\Theta(n^4)$</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375590\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Determine the complexity of the following code, using “$\\Theta$” as indicated.</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i *= 2)\n    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= i; j++)\n        sum++;\n        print(sum);\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\Theta \\left(n^{2}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\Theta\\left(n^{2} \\log n\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\Theta(n)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"375621\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer : Option C</strong></p><p>Unroll the inner for loop .<br><br>Time complexity will be $1+2+4+.....+2^{j}$$= \\Theta \\left ( 2^{j} \\right )= \\Theta \\left ( n \\right )$&nbsp;where $j= \\log _{2}n$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"375778\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer to this question is option C<br>we will see why<br><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/635635b397cb204d18cc.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"503875\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the code analysis, the correct answer is <b>C. $\\Theta(n)$</b>.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"1\"><b>Step-by-Step Analysis</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"2\">To determine the time complexity, we need to count the total number of times the inner statement <code>sum++;</code> is executed.</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b>Analyze the Outer Loop:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1,0,0\">The loop variable <code>i</code> starts at $1$ and multiplies by $2$ in each iteration (<code>i *= 2</code>).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1,1,0\">The loop continues as long as <code>i &lt;= n</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1,2,0\">The values of <code>i</code> will be powers of 2: $1, 2, 4, 8, \\dots, 2^k$, where $2^k \\le n$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,1,3,0\">The number of iterations for the outer loop is roughly $\\log_2 n$.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b>Analyze the Inner Loop:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,1,0,0\">For a given value of <code>i</code>, the inner loop runs from $j = 1$ to $j = i$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,1,1,0\">This means the inner loop executes exactly <b>$i$ times</b> for each iteration of the outer loop.</p></li></ul></li><li><p>Calculate Total Executions:<br>We sum the number of inner loop executions for each value of i:<br>&nbsp;<br>$$Total \\ Operations = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + \\dots + 2^k$$<br>This is a geometric series where the common ratio is $2$. The sum of a geometric series is given by the formula:<br>&nbsp;<br>$$Sum = a \\frac{r^m - 1}{r - 1}$$<br>&nbsp;<br>Where:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,2,5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,5,0,0\">$a = 1$ (first term)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,5,1,0\">$r = 2$ (common ratio)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,5,2,0\">$m$ is the number of terms (which is $k+1$)</p></li></ul><p>So, the sum is:<br>&nbsp;<br>$$Sum = 2^{k+1} - 1$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,0\"><b>Relate to $n$:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1,0,0\">We know that the loop stops when <code>i</code> exceeds <code>n</code>. The largest value of <code>i</code> (which is $2^k$) is the largest power of 2 less than or equal to $n$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1,1,0\">Therefore, $2^k \\le n &lt; 2^{k+1}$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1,2,0\">The total sum $2^{k+1} - 1$ is roughly equal to $2 \\cdot 2^k$, which is approximately $2n$ in the worst case.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,1,3,0\">Mathematically, since $n/2 &lt; 2^k \\le n$, the total operations count is between $n$ and $2n$.</p></li></ul></li></ol><h3 data-path-to-node=\"4\"><b>Conclusion</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">Since the total number of operations is linearly proportional to $n$ (specifically bounded between $n$ and $2n$), the tightest asymptotic bound is linear.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\"><b>Complexity = $\\Theta(n)$</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"7\">&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375589\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider functions $f()$ and $g()$ which are positive increasing functions.</p><p>Which of the following(s) is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $f(n)=o(g(n))$ then $\\log (f(n))=o(\\log (g(n))$, here $o$ is small-oh</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $f(n)=\\mathrm{O}(g(n))$ then $e^{f(n)}=\\mathrm{O}\\left(e^{g(n)}\\right)$, here $\\mathrm{O}$ is big-oh</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $f(n)=O(g(n))$ then $\\log (f(n))=O(\\log (g(n))$, here $O$ is big-oh</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $f(n)=o(g(n))$ then $e^{f(n)}=o\\left(e^{g(n)}\\right)$, here $o$ is small-oh</p>"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"375636\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Lets analyze each options one by one :-</p>\n\n<p>Option A :-&nbsp; &nbsp;Given&nbsp; $f(n)=o(g(n))$&nbsp; &nbsp;means&nbsp; $f(n) &lt; g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now if we take $log$ on both the sides and compare <strong>asymptotically</strong>&nbsp;then two cases can be possible :-</p>\n\n<p>case 1 :-&nbsp; $log(f(n)) &lt; log(g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; ex :-&nbsp; $f(n)=n^{logn}$&nbsp; and&nbsp; &nbsp;$g(n)=n^{n}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logf(n)=(logn)^{2}$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logg(n)=nlogn$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; so we got&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$log(f(n)) &lt; log(g(n)$</p>\n\n<p>case 2 :-&nbsp;$log(f(n)) = log(g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;ex :-&nbsp; $f(n)=n!$&nbsp; and&nbsp; &nbsp;$g(n)=n^{n}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logf(n)=log(n!)=nlogn$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logg(n)=nlogn$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; so we got&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$log(f(n)) =&nbsp;log(g(n)$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hence option A is incorrect.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option B :-&nbsp; &nbsp;Given&nbsp; $f(n)=O(g(n))$</p>\n\n<p>Lets take $f(n)=n$&nbsp; and&nbsp; $g(n)=\\frac{n}{2}$</p>\n\n<p>So&nbsp; &nbsp;$e^{f(n)}=e^n$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;and&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $e^{g(n)}=e^{\\frac{n}{2}}=(\\sqrt{e})^{n}$</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore$ from above we get&nbsp;&nbsp;$e^{f(n)}=\\Omega (e^{g(n)})$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hence option B is incorrect.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option C :-&nbsp; &nbsp;Given&nbsp; $f(n)=O(g(n))$&nbsp; &nbsp;means&nbsp; $f(n) \\leq&nbsp;g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now if we take $log$ on both the sides and compare <strong>asymptotically</strong>&nbsp;then two cases can be possible :-</p>\n\n<p>case 1 :-&nbsp; $log(f(n)) &lt; log(g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; ex :-&nbsp; $f(n)=n^{n}$&nbsp; and&nbsp; &nbsp;$g(n)=n^{logn}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logf(n)=nlogn$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logg(n)=(logn)^{2}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; so we got&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$log(f(n)) &lt; log(g(n)$</p>\n\n<p>case 2 :-&nbsp;$log(f(n)) = log(g(n)$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;ex :-&nbsp; $f(n)=n^{n}$&nbsp; and&nbsp; &nbsp;$g(n)=n!$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logf(n)=nlogn$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $logg(n)=log(n!)=nlogn$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; so we got&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$log(f(n)) =&nbsp;log(g(n)$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hence option C&nbsp;is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option D :-&nbsp;&nbsp;Given&nbsp; $f(n)=o(g(n))$</p>\n\n<p>Lets take $f(n)=n$&nbsp; and&nbsp; $g(n)=n^{2}$</p>\n\n<p>So&nbsp; &nbsp;$e^{f(n)}=e^n$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;and&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $e^{g(n)}=e^{n^{2}}$</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore$ from above we get&nbsp;&nbsp;$e^{f(n)}=o(e^{f(n)})$</p>\n\n<p>this will always hold as higher the power of exponential function higher is the growth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hence option D&nbsp;is correct.</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"502761\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"760\" src=\"media/6d/6d9e008d0debc287c812.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375588\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following(s) is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $f(n)=n \\log n, g(n)=\\displaystyle{}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{n}{i}$ then $f(n)=\\Theta(g(n))$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $f(n)=1.01^{n}, g(n)=n^{1000}$ then $f(n)=\\Omega(g(n))$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $f(n)=\\displaystyle{}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i^{2}, g(n)=n^{3}+\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i$ then $f(n)=\\Theta(g(n))$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $f(n)=n !, g(n)=3^{n} \\times 5^{n}$ then $f(n)=\\Omega(g(n))$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"375798\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer to this question are&nbsp;options A,B,C,D</p>\n\n<p>now we will see why</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb73b5bad2ca46f7aa7c.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"375620\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer : A , B , C , D</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Option A:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Given $f\\left (n \\right )= n\\log n$ ,&nbsp;$g\\left ( n \\right )= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{n}{i}= n\\left ( 1+2+....+\\frac{1}{n} \\right )= n\\log n= \\Theta \\left ( g\\left ( n \\right ) \\right )$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option B:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Applying log &nbsp;to&nbsp;$f\\left ( n \\right ),g\\left ( n \\right )$ and removing constants we get&nbsp;$n&gt; \\log n \\Rightarrow f\\left ( n \\right )= \\Omega \\left ( g\\left ( n \\right ) \\right )$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option C:</strong></p>\n\n<p>$f(n)= O\\left ( n^{3} \\right ),g(n)= O(n^{3})\\Rightarrow f(n)= \\Theta \\left ( g\\left ( n \\right ) \\right )$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option D:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Applying log &nbsp;to&nbsp;$f\\left ( n \\right ),g\\left ( n \\right )$ and removing constants we get&nbsp;$\\log n&gt; 1$$\\Rightarrow f(n)= \\Omega \\left ( g\\left ( n \\right ) \\right )$</p>\n\n<p>$\\therefore$ <strong>All Options are correct</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"531996\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"764\" height=\"1168\" src=\"media/67/67979d545f2652a92ebd.jpg\"></p><p>at least 5 character</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375587\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statement is FALSE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $f(n)=\\mathcal{O}(g(n))$ and $f(n)=\\Omega(g(n))$, then we have $(f(n))^{2}= \\Theta\\left((g(n))^{2}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $f(n)=\\mathcal{O}(g(n))$ and $f(n)=\\Omega(g(n))$, then we have $f(n)=g(n)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$2^{n}+n^{2}=\\mathcal{O}\\left(3^{n}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$2^{n}+n^{2}=\\mathcal{O}\\left(2^{n}\\right)$&nbsp;</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 11)</small></h4><a name=\"375484\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer: B</p><p>Option A: $f(n) = O(g(n))$ and $f(n) = \\Omega(g(n)) \\implies f(n) = \\Theta(g(n)))$.Which means $(f(n))^2 = \\Theta((g(n))^2)$. Thus, <strong>A is True, and not the answer.</strong></p><p>Option B:&nbsp;$f(n) = O(g(n))$ and $f(n) = \\Omega(g(n)) \\implies f(n) = \\Theta(g(n)))$. Note that $f$ and $g$ are only asymptotically equal, they need not be the same functions, i.e, $f(n) = g(n)$ need not be true. Thus, <strong>B is False, and is the answer.</strong></p><p>Option C: $2^n + n^2 \\lt 3^n$ asymptotically. Thus, <strong>C is True, and is not the answer.</strong></p><p>Option D:&nbsp;$2^n + n^2 \\leq&nbsp;2^n$ asymptotically. This, <strong>D is True, and is not the answer.</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375457\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume you have two positive functions $f$ and $g$ such that $f(n)$ is in $O(g(n))$. For each of the following statements, decide which one(s) is/are always TRUE.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$2^{f(n)}$ is $O\\left(2^{g(n)}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$f(n)^{2}$ is $O\\left(g(n)^{2}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$f(n)=O\\left((f(n))^{2}\\right)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$g(n)=\\Omega(g(n))$</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"375546\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Lets explore each options one by one :-</p>\n\n<p>Option A :-&nbsp; &nbsp; Lets consider $f(n)&nbsp;= n$&nbsp; and&nbsp;&nbsp;$g(n)=\\frac{n}{2}$&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>so $f(n)$ and $g(n)$ asymptotically holds the relation&nbsp;as :-</p>\n\n<h3>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<strong>&nbsp; </strong>$f(n) = O (g(n))$</h3>\n\n<p>now ,</p>\n\n<p>$2^{f(n)}=2^{n}$&nbsp; &nbsp; and&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$2^{g(n)}=2^{\\frac{n}{2}}$&nbsp; =&nbsp;&nbsp;$(\\sqrt{2})^{n}$</p>\n\n<p>therefore the given statement&nbsp; &nbsp; $2^{f(n)}=O(2^{g(n)})$&nbsp; &nbsp;<strong>Does not hold hence incorrect option.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option B :-&nbsp; &nbsp; Lets take $f(n)=n^{2}$ and&nbsp; $g(n) = n^{3}$</p>\n\n<p>so $f(n)$ and $g(n)$ asymptotically holds the relation&nbsp;as :-</p>\n\n<h3>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<strong>&nbsp; </strong>$f(n) = O (g(n))$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;-----eq(1)</h3>\n\n<p>now ,</p>\n\n<p>if we multiply $f(n)$ on LHS of eq(1) and multiply $g(n)$ on RHS fof eq(1) we get :-</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; $(f(n))^{2} = n^{4}$&nbsp; and&nbsp; $(g(n))^{2} = n^{6}$</p>\n\n<p>so we noticed that if growth of $g(n)$ is more than $f(n)$ then growth of $(g(n))^{2}$ will also be greater than $(f(n))^{2}$ , Hence the relation&nbsp;$(f(n))^{2} = O(g(n))^{2}$&nbsp;<strong>holds and option is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option C:-&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Lets take&nbsp;$f(n)=\\frac{1}{n}$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>so $(f(n))^{2}=\\frac{1}{n^2}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;given :-&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $f(n) &lt;= c.(f(n))^{2}$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $\\frac{\\frac{1}{n}}{\\frac{1}{(n)^{2}}}&lt;= c$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $n &lt;= c$&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Hence the relation $f(n)=O(f(n))^{2}$ <strong>does not hold so the option is incorrect.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Option D:-&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The relation&nbsp;&nbsp;$g(n)=\\Omega (g(n))$ is trivial and it is always true.<strong>Hence the option is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Answer :-&nbsp; B ,D</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"499303\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the analysis of the asymptotic notation properties, the statements that are always TRUE are <b>B</b> and <b>D</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">Here is the step-by-step evaluation of each option:</p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\"><b>Given:</b></p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\">$f(n)$ and $g(n)$ are positive functions.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\">$f(n) = O(g(n))$, which means there exist constants $c &gt; 0, n_0$ such that $f(n) \\le c \\cdot g(n)$ for all $n \\ge n_0$.</p></li></ol><hr data-path-to-node=\"4\"><p data-path-to-node=\"5\"><b>A. $2^{f(n)}$ is $O(2^{g(n)})$ — FALSE</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\">Big-O notation does not generally preserve order when exponentiated because a constant factor in the exponent becomes a power of the base.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\"><li><p>Proof by Counter-example:</p><p>Let $f(n) = 2n$ and $g(n) = n$.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,0,0\">Is $2n = O(n)$? <b>Yes</b>, simply choose $c \\ge 2$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,1,0\">Now check the exponentials: $2^{f(n)} = 2^{2n} = 4^n$ and $2^{g(n)} = 2^n$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,2,0\">Is $4^n = O(2^n)$? <b>No</b>. $\\frac{4^n}{2^n} = 2^n$, which grows to infinity. There is no constant $k$ such that $4^n \\le k \\cdot 2^n$.</p></li></ul></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=\"8\"><p data-path-to-node=\"9\"><b>B. $f(n)^2$ is $O(g(n)^2)$ — TRUE</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"10\">Squaring both sides of an inequality preserves the order for positive numbers.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"11\"><li><p>Proof:</p><p>From the given condition, we have:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$f(n) \\le c \\cdot g(n)$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Since both functions are positive, we can square both sides:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$f(n)^2 \\le (c \\cdot g(n))^2$$</p><p>$$f(n)^2 \\le c^2 \\cdot g(n)^2$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Let $k = c^2$. Since $c$ is a constant, $k$ is also a constant.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$f(n)^2 \\le k \\cdot g(n)^2$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This inequality perfectly matches the definition of Big-O notation.</p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=\"12\"><p data-path-to-node=\"13\"><b>C. $f(n) = O((f(n))^2)$ — FALSE</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"14\">This statement implies that $f(n)$ must be bounded below by a constant (i.e., it cannot approach zero).</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"15\"><li><p>Proof by Counter-example:</p><p>Let $f(n) = \\frac{1}{n}$. This is a positive function.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"15,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,1,0,0\">Is $\\frac{1}{n} = O((\\frac{1}{n})^2)$?</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,1,1,0\">Check the inequality: $\\frac{1}{n} \\le c \\cdot \\frac{1}{n^2}$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,1,2,0\">Multiply by $n^2$: $n \\le c$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,1,3,0\">This is <b>False</b> for large $n$. A function that decays to zero (like $\\frac{1}{n}$) grows <i>faster</i> than its square, so it cannot be Big-O of its square.</p></li></ul></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=\"16\"><p data-path-to-node=\"17\"><b>D. $g(n) = \\Omega(g(n))$ — TRUE</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"18\">This is the <b>reflexive property</b> of asymptotic notation. Every function is asymptotically lower-bounded by itself.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"19\"><li><p>Proof:</p><p>By definition, $g(n) = \\Omega(g(n))$ if there exists a constant $c &gt; 0$ such that $g(n) \\ge c \\cdot g(n)$.</p><p>Simply choose $c = 1$.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$g(n) \\ge 1 \\cdot g(n)$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>This is trivially true for all values of $n$.</p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=\"20\"><p>Conclusion:</p><p>The correct statements are B and D.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375450\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{S}_{1}= \\displaystyle{}\\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n-1} \\frac{n r}{2^{r}}$, and $\\text{S}_{2} = \\displaystyle{} \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n-1} r 2^{r}$.<br>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{S}_{1}=\\Theta(n \\log n), \\text{S}_{2}=\\Theta(n \\log n)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{S}_{1}=\\Theta(n), \\text{S}_{2}=\\Theta(n \\log n)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{S}_{1}=\\Theta(n \\log n), \\text{S}_{2}=\\Theta(n)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{S}_{1}=\\Theta(n), \\text{S}_{2}=\\Theta(n)$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 26)</small></h4><a name=\"375514\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>complexities of respective series S1 and S2 can be calculated as follows :-</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b43b3ed10399fc0ef956.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb7d70987bf4930aab55.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"498761\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>QUICK WAY TO SOLVE</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"1181\" height=\"1280\" src=\"media/a0/a0c6582f1eb89fb0b415.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"499301\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">To evaluate the asymptotic complexity of the given summations, let's analyze them one by one.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Analyzing $S_1$</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_1 = \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n - 1} \\frac{nr}{2^r}$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"3\">Since $n$ is a constant with respect to the summation variable $r$, we can factor it out:</p><p>$$S_1 = n \\cdot \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n - 1} \\frac{r}{2^r}$$</p><p>Let's look at the summation part: $\\sum \\frac{r}{2^r} = \\sum r \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^r$.</p><p>This is an Arithmetico-Geometric Progression (AGP) where the geometric ratio is $x = \\frac{1}{2}$.</p><p>Since $|x| &lt; 1$, the infinite series $\\sum_{r=0}^{\\infty} r x^r$ converges to a constant value. Specifically, for $x = 1/2$, it converges to 2.</p><p>Since the infinite sum converges to a constant, the partial sum up to $\\log n - 1$ is bounded by a constant (specifically $\\Theta(1)$).</p><p>Therefore:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_1 = n \\cdot \\Theta(1) = \\Theta(n)$$</p><hr data-path-to-node=\"9\"><p data-path-to-node=\"10\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Analyzing $S_2$</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_2 = \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n - 1} r 2^r$$</p><p>This is also an Arithmetico-Geometric Progression (AGP) with the geometric term being $2^r$. Here, the common ratio is $x = 2$.</p><p>Since $|x| &gt; 1$, the terms grow exponentially, and the sum will be dominated by the last term in the series.</p><p>Let $k = \\log n - 1$. The largest term is roughly when $r = k$.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$\\text{Last term} \\approx k \\cdot 2^k$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Substituting $k = \\log n - 1$:</p><p>$$ \\approx (\\log n - 1) \\cdot 2^{\\log n - 1}$$</p><p>$$ \\approx (\\log n) \\cdot \\frac{2^{\\log n}}{2}$$</p><p>Since $2^{\\log n} = n$:</p><p>$$ \\approx \\frac{1}{2} n \\log n$$</p><p>Let's verify this formally with the summation formula for $\\sum_{i=1}^{k} i x^i$:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$\\sum_{r=0}^{k} r x^r = \\frac{x(1 - (k+1)x^k + kx^{k+1})}{(1-x)^2}$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For $x=2$:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_2 = 2 \\cdot (1 - (k+1)2^k + k2^{k+1})$$</p><p>$$S_2 \\approx 2 \\cdot k \\cdot 2^{k+1}$$</p><p>$$S_2 \\approx k \\cdot 2^{k+2}$$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Substituting $k \\approx \\log n$:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_2 \\approx \\log n \\cdot 2^{\\log n} = n \\log n$$</p><p>Therefore, the growth rate is dominated by $n \\log n$:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$S_2 = \\Theta(n \\log n)$$</p><hr data-path-to-node=\"26\"><p data-path-to-node=\"27\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Conclusion</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"28\">We have found:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"29\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"29,0,0\">$S_1 = \\Theta(n)$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"29,1,0\">$S_2 = \\Theta(n \\log n)$</p></li></ul><p>Comparing this with the given options:</p><p>A. $S_1 = \\Theta(n \\log n), S_2 = \\Theta(n \\log n)$</p><p>B. $S_1 = \\Theta(n), S_2 = \\Theta(n \\log n)$</p><p>C. $S_1 = \\Theta(n \\log n), S_2 = \\Theta(n)$</p><p>D. $S_1 = \\Theta(n), S_2 = \\Theta(n)$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"31\">The correct option is <b>B</b>.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"378879\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>I miss understood option 1: <strong>True way</strong></p>\n\n<p>n(½&nbsp;+ ¼&nbsp;+ 1/8&nbsp;+ 1/16 + …. + 1/2^(logN)) = N*( ½&nbsp;* [1 –&nbsp;½&nbsp;^(logN)]/(½)) = N * [1 –&nbsp;1/N] = N ≈&nbsp;O(N)&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500729\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let&nbsp;<br>\\[<br>S_1 = \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n - 1} \\frac{nr}{2^r},&nbsp;<br>\\qquad&nbsp;<br>S_2 = \\sum_{r=0}^{\\log n - 1} r 2^r.<br>\\]</p><p>Let \\(k = \\log n\\). &nbsp;<br>So the sums run from \\(0\\) to \\(k - 1\\).</p><hr><p>\\[<br>S_1&nbsp;<br>= n\\left(&nbsp;<br>\\frac{0}{2^0}&nbsp;<br>+ \\frac{1}{2^1}&nbsp;<br>+ \\frac{2}{2^2}&nbsp;<br>+ \\frac{3}{2^3}&nbsp;<br>+ \\cdots&nbsp;<br>+ \\frac{k}{2^k}&nbsp;<br>\\right)<br>\\]</p><p>Look at this sequence:</p><p>- Term 1: \\(0\\) &nbsp;<br>- Term 2: \\(1/2\\) &nbsp;<br>- Term 3: \\(2/4 = 1/2\\) &nbsp;<br>- Term 4: \\(3/8 = 0.375\\) &nbsp;<br>- Term 5: \\(4/16 = 0.25\\) &nbsp;<br>- Term 6: \\(5/32\\), etc.</p><p>The terms keep getting smaller. &nbsp;<br>It is a rapidly decreasing series — the sum never exceeds \\(2\\) (in fact it converges to \\(2\\)).</p><p>So the inside part satisfies:</p><p>\\[<br>\\sum_{r=0}^{k} \\frac{r}{2^r} = \\Theta(1).<br>\\]</p><p>Hence,</p><p>\\[<br>S_1 = n \\times (\\text{constant}) = \\Theta(n).<br>\\]</p><hr><p>Now consider:</p><p>\\[<br>S_2 = 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 4 + 3 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 16 + \\cdots + (\\log n-1) \\cdot 2^{\\log n-1}<br>\\]</p><p>Observe how the terms behave:</p><p>- \\(1 \\cdot 2 = 2\\) &nbsp;<br>- \\(2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\) &nbsp;<br>- \\(3 \\cdot 8 = 24\\) &nbsp;<br>- \\(4 \\cdot 16 = 64\\) &nbsp;<br>...</p><p>Last term:</p><p>\\[<br>(\\log n)\\cdot 2^{\\log n}.<br>\\]</p><p>Using log property</p><p>\\[<br>2^{\\log n} = n^{\\log 2} = n<br>\\]</p><p>Thus the last term is approximately:</p><p>\\[<br>(\\log n)\\cdot n = n\\log n.<br>\\]</p><p>Since this is an increasing series, the largest term dominates the whole sum. Therefore,</p><p>\\[<br>S_2 = \\Theta(n \\log n).<br>\\]</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375449\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Match Column A with Column B. Column B represents asymptotic time complexity of loops in Column A<br>$$\\begin{array}{|l|l|}<br>\\hline \\qquad&nbsp;\\quad \\textbf {Column A} &amp; \\textbf {Column B} \\\\<br>\\hline a) \\quad&nbsp;{\\color{Purple} {\\textbf{for} }}&nbsp;\\left(\\mathrm{i}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{i}^{*} \\mathrm{i}&lt;=\\mathrm{N} ; \\mathrm{i}={\\color{Green} {2}}^{*} \\mathrm{i}\\right) ; &amp; &nbsp;\\mathrm{i}) \\quad \\Theta(\\log N) \\\\<br>\\hline &nbsp;b) \\quad {\\color{Purple} {\\textbf{for} }} \\left(\\mathrm{i}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{i}&lt;=\\mathrm{N} ; \\mathrm{i}={\\color{Green} {2}}^{*} \\mathrm{i}\\right) &nbsp;&amp; &nbsp;\\mathrm{ii}) \\quad \\Theta(\\mathrm{N}) \\\\<br>\\quad \\qquad {\\color{Teal} {\\textbf{for} }} (\\mathrm{j}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{j}&lt;=\\mathrm{i} ; \\mathrm{j}=\\mathrm{j}+{\\color{Blue} {1}}) ; &amp; \\\\<br>\\hline c) \\quad {\\color{Purple} {\\textbf{for} }} \\left(\\mathrm{i}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{i}{ }^{*} \\mathrm{i}&lt;=\\mathrm{N} ; \\mathrm{i}=\\mathrm{i}+{\\color{Blue} {1}}\\right) &nbsp;&amp; \\mathrm{iii}) \\quad \\Theta(\\sqrt{N} \\log N) \\\\<br>\\qquad \\quad {\\color{Teal} {\\textbf{for} }} (\\mathrm{j}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{j}&lt;=\\mathrm{i} ; \\mathrm{j}=\\mathrm{j}+{\\color{Blue} {1}}) ; &amp; \\\\<br>\\hline d) \\quad {\\color{Purple} {\\textbf{for} }} \\left(\\mathrm{i}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{i}{ }^{*} \\mathrm{i}&lt;=\\mathrm{N} ; \\mathrm{i}=\\mathrm{i}+{\\color{Blue} {1}}\\right)&nbsp;&amp; iv) \\quad \\Theta(N \\log \\mathrm{N}) \\\\<br>\\qquad \\quad {\\color{Teal} {\\textbf{for} }} \\left(\\mathrm{j}={\\color{Blue} {1}} ; \\mathrm{j}&lt;=\\mathrm{i} ; \\mathrm{j}={\\color{Green} {2}}^{*} \\mathrm{j}\\right) ; &amp; \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}$$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{a-i, b-ii, c-ii, d-iii}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 30)</small></h4><a name=\"375523\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer is Option C</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/816c347f9e773cf706a0.jpg\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/95/956151c8af7a3a2de0e5.jpg\"></strong></p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2e001538c77f656d3201.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"378832\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let me tell you option B: ”jiski vajha se mere 2 marks cut ho gye”</p>\n\n<p>We have&nbsp; sequence as: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + …. log(N) =&nbsp;1.(2^(logN) –&nbsp;1)/1 = 2^(logN) = N ≈&nbsp;O(N) : Option (ii)</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lession : “Always make sequence &amp; then check for pattern : naki&nbsp;question dekha&nbsp;nahi ki answer tik kardiya”</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"499306\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the step-by-step analysis of each loop's execution, here is the solution:</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\"><b>1. Analyzing Loop (a):</b></p><p>C</p><p><button aria-label=\"Copy code\"></button></p><pre><code role=\"text\" data-test-id=\"code-content\">for (i = 1; i*i &lt;= N; i = 2*i);\n</code></pre><ul data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b>Condition:</b> The loop runs as long as $i^2 \\le N$, or $i \\le \\sqrt{N}$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b>Update:</b> The variable <code>i</code> doubles in each iteration ($1, 2, 4, 8, \\dots$).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,0\"><b>Iterations:</b> The number of steps is the number of times you can multiply 1 by 2 before it exceeds $\\sqrt{N}$. This is $\\log_2(\\sqrt{N})$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,0\">Using logarithm properties, $\\log_2(\\sqrt{N}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2 N$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,4,0\"><b>Complexity:</b> $\\Theta(\\log N)$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,5,0\"><b>Matches:</b> <b>(i)</b></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><b>2. Analyzing Loop (b):</b></p><p>C</p><p><button aria-label=\"Copy code\"></button></p><pre><code role=\"text\" data-test-id=\"code-content\">for (i = 1; i &lt;= N; i = 2*i)\n    for(j = 1; j &lt;= i; j = j + 1);\n</code></pre><ul data-path-to-node=\"6\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,0,0\"><b>Outer Loop:</b> <code>i</code> takes values $1, 2, 4, 8, \\dots$ up to $N$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,0\"><b>Inner Loop:</b> Runs <code>i</code> times for each value of <code>i</code>.</p></li><li><p>Total Work: Sum of the inner loop iterations.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + \\dots + N$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,3,0\">This is a geometric series sum. For a series ending at $N$ (where $N$ is a power of 2), the sum is $2N - 1$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,4,0\"><b>Complexity:</b> $\\Theta(N)$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,5,0\"><b>Matches:</b> <b>(ii)</b></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"7\"><b>3. Analyzing Loop (c):</b></p><p>C</p><p><button aria-label=\"Copy code\"></button></p><pre><code role=\"text\" data-test-id=\"code-content\">for (i = 1; i*i &lt;= N; i = i + 1)\n    for(j = 1; j &lt;= i; j = j + 1);\n</code></pre><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\"><b>Outer Loop:</b> The loop runs for $i = 1, 2, 3, \\dots$ until $i &gt; \\sqrt{N}$. So, the outer loop runs $\\sqrt{N}$ times.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\"><b>Inner Loop:</b> Runs <code>i</code> times.</p></li><li><p>Total Work: Sum of arithmetic series from $1$ to $\\sqrt{N}$.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$1 + 2 + 3 + \\dots + \\sqrt{N} = \\frac{\\sqrt{N}(\\sqrt{N} + 1)}{2} \\approx \\frac{(\\sqrt{N})^2}{2} = \\frac{N}{2}$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,0\"><b>Complexity:</b> $\\Theta(N)$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,4,0\"><b>Matches:</b> <b>(ii)</b></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"10\"><b>4. Analyzing Loop (d):</b></p><p>C</p><p><button aria-label=\"Copy code\"></button></p><pre><code role=\"text\" data-test-id=\"code-content\">for (i = 1; i*i &lt;= N; i = i + 1)\n    for(j = 1; j &lt;= i; j = 2*j);\n</code></pre><ul data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\"><b>Outer Loop:</b> Runs for $i = 1$ to $\\sqrt{N}$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\"><b>Inner Loop:</b> Variable <code>j</code> doubles until it reaches <code>i</code>. This takes $\\log_2 i$ steps.</p></li><li><p>Total Work: Sum of logarithms.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$\\sum_{i=1}^{\\sqrt{N}} \\log_2 i = \\log_2(1) + \\log_2(2) + \\dots + \\log_2(\\sqrt{N})$$</p><p>$$= \\log_2((\\sqrt{N})!)$$</p></li><li><p>Using Stirling's approximation ($\\log(n!) \\approx n \\log n$):</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$\\approx \\sqrt{N} \\log(\\sqrt{N}) = \\sqrt{N} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\log N$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,4,0\"><b>Complexity:</b> $\\Theta(\\sqrt{N} \\log N)$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,5,0\"><b>Matches:</b> <b>(iii)</b></p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=\"13\"><p data-path-to-node=\"14\"><b>Matching Results:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"15\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\">(a) $\\rightarrow$ (i)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\">(b) $\\rightarrow$ (ii)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,2,0\">(c) $\\rightarrow$ (ii)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,0\">(d) $\\rightarrow$ (iii)</p></li></ul><p>Checking the given options:</p><p>A. a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv</p><p>B. a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii</p><p>C. a-i, b-ii, c-ii, d-iii</p><p>D. None of these</p><p data-path-to-node=\"17\">The correct option is <b>C</b>.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375443\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":20.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":10}],"stats":{"questions":10,"maxMarks":18,"types":{"MSQ":4,"MCQ":6},"withoutKey":1}};
